# Findings reviewed and deliberately not fixed yet. Every entry carries an
# expiry date: on that date Trivy stops honouring it and the scan fails again,
# which forces a fresh decision instead of letting an acceptance rot in place.
#
# CVE-2026-49272  oauthlib, SocialOSINT image. Not reachable here.
#
#   The vulnerable code is DeviceCodeGrant.validate_token_request, in the
#   authorization-SERVER half of oauthlib: it is reached when oauthlib is used
#   to run a token endpoint (oauthlib/oauth2/rfc6749/endpoints/ and
#   rfc8628/grant_types/). This image only ever acts as an OAuth client.
#   Nothing in it instantiates oauthlib's Endpoints or Server classes, and
#   requests_oauthlib 2.0.0 imports only client-side symbols
#   (WebApplicationClient, LegacyApplicationClient, oauthlib.oauth1 constants);
#   tweepy touches oauthlib.oauth1.Client only in its asynchronous client,
#   which SocialOSINT does not use.
#
#   Verify with:
#     docker run --rm --entrypoint sh <image> -c \
#       'grep -rn "DeviceCodeGrant\|oauthlib.oauth2.Server" \
#          /usr/local/lib/python3.11/site-packages/'
#
#   Upgrading is separate and blocked: the fix is 4.0.0 and tweepy 4.17
#   declares oauthlib<4. That is a metadata conflict, not the reason for this
#   entry: forcing past it would add risk to clear a finding that cannot fire.
#   Re-examine if the app ever validates OAuth2 grants itself, or when tweepy
#   relaxes the pin (`pip install --dry-run "tweepy" oauthlib==4.0.0`).
CVE-2026-49272 exp:2027-04-30
